sorting through the wreckage after a  deadly tornado ripped through Lee County  in southeast Alabama alongside the  cleanup effort an urgent search for the  missing there are areas I understand  they still have not been able to get  into yet and search the rubble there are  adults as well as children involved how  many I cannot tell you at this time  authorities do know that more than 20  people were killed when the storm hit on  Sunday winds of around a hundred and 60  miles per hour flattening homes snapping  trees and powerlines the looming funnel  cloud clearly visible from the  neighboring state of Georgia words  cannot describe it  trailer homes turned upside down damaged  this is unbelievable  it just took pine trees and cut them in  half telephone poles cut them in half at  the school it took a 12-ton air  conditioner picking nothing through it  at 40 feet some like this woman are  lucky to have escaped at all but now she  and many others face a colossal  challenge the trail of destruction the  tornado left behind is at points half a  mile wide and there's little comfort to  be found here as residents try to come  to terms with what's happened  the basic cleaner has already begun but  it will be a long time before this small  community is back to normal  Hanna Thomas Peter Sky News in New York
 

